Source: https://github.com/cactus-compute/needle

Used as the router's semantic-fact embedder (docs/ROUTING.md §4): a fixed 35 MB feature
extractor; all tunable routing intelligence stays in this repo's own config. How it reaches
each platform: prebuilt static engines (`libneedle.a` for ios-arm64, ios-sim-arm64 and
android-arm64) plus the `needle3.cact` weights, fetched from the upstream HuggingFace repo
(`Cactus-Compute/needle3`) by `fetch_needle.sh` at setup time and never committed or
redistributed by this repository — the same policy as the iOS LiteRT-LM frameworks. The
binaries are unmodified. Upstream publishes no NOTICE file, so there is no notice text to
reproduce.

The engine's Python-side CLI documents telemetry on by default; the C engines linked here
were verified network-silent on macOS via strings/lsof probing (2026-09-20), with
real-device verification tracked in FUTURE_ENHANCEMENTS.md before any release ships them.

## Embedded Python runtime and packages (iOS and Android)

Both mobile apps embed a CPython 3.10 runtime and a set of third-party Python packages, so
that model-requested tool calls (`run_python`, charting, QR codes, astronomy, image editing)
execute on-device rather than on a server.

CPython and its standard library are used under the Python Software Foundation License.

On Android the runtime is provided by **Chaquopy**, © Chaquo Ltd and contributors, licensed
under the MIT License (https://github.com/chaquo/chaquopy). Chaquopy has been open source
since version 12.0.1; no license key is required. Packages are declared in
`AIAndroid/app/build.gradle.kts` and installed at build time: numpy, pandas, scipy,
matplotlib, plotly, astral, folium, shapely, qrcode and sympy, plus their dependencies.

On iOS the runtime is built separately (mobile-forge) and embedded as `Python.xcframework`
with packages under `app_packages/` — 29 distributions covering the same feature set, minus
scipy and shapely.

All of these packages are under permissive licenses — BSD 2- and 3-Clause, MIT, MIT-CMU,
Apache 2.0, and the PSF and matplotlib licenses. None restricts commercial or private use.

**Each package's own license text ships alongside it**, in its `.dist-info/` directory inside
the app bundle (for example `numpy-*.dist-info/LICENSE.txt`; matplotlib additionally carries
eleven separate license files for its bundled fonts and color maps). That is how the
retain-the-copyright-notice condition these licenses impose is satisfied at distribution time
— the notices travel with the binaries rather than being reproduced here, since the exact set
and versions change whenever the dependency set is rebuilt.
